    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the three common representations.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE — adjacency list:\n```ts\ntype Graph = number[][];\n\nfunction buildAdjacencyList(\n  vertexCount: number,\n  edges: readonly [number, number][],\n  directed = false,\n): Graph {\n  const graph: number[][] = [];\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < vertexCount; i++) {\n    graph.push([]);\n  }\n\n  for (const [from, to] of edges) {\n    graph[from]!.push(to);\n\n    if (!directed) {\n      graph[to]!.push(from);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return graph;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN — Map-based adjacency list:\n```ts\nfunction buildGraphUsingMap(\n  edges: readonly [string, string][],\n): Map<string, string[]> {\n  const graph = new Map<string, string[]>();\n\n  for (const [from, to] of edges) {\n    if (!graph.has(from)) graph.set(from, []);\n    graph.get(from)!.push(to);\n  }\n\n  return graph;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\nEdges:\nA-B, A-C, B-D\n\nAdjacency list:\nA → [B,C]\nB → [A,D]\nC → [A]\nD → [B]\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\nAdjacency list storage = O(V + E).\nAdjacency matrix storage = O(V²).\nNeighbor iteration with an adjacency list is O(degree(v)).\n\nStep 4 — Interview rule:\nStart from the operation:\n- Need neighbors → adjacency list.\n- Need O(1) edge-existence checks in a dense graph → matrix.\n- Need to process all edges → edge list can be enough.",
