    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Recursive DFS.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction dfsRecursive(\n  start: number,\n  graph: readonly (readonly number[])[],\n): number[] {\n  const visited = new Array<boolean>(graph.length).fill(false);\n  const order: number[] = [];\n\n  function visit(node: number): void {\n    visited[node] = true;\n    order.push(node);\n\n    for (const next of graph[node] ?? []) {\n      if (!visited[next]) {\n        visit(next);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  visit(start);\n  return order;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN — explicit stack:\n```ts\nfunction dfsIterative(\n  start: number,\n  graph: readonly (readonly number[])[],\n): number[] {\n  const visited = new Set<number>();\n  const stack: number[] = [start];\n  const order: number[] = [];\n\n  while (stack.length > 0) {\n    const node = stack.pop()!;\n\n    if (visited.has(node)) continue;\n\n    visited.add(node);\n    order.push(node);\n\n    const neighbors = graph[node] ?? [];\n\n    for (let i = neighbors.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n      if (!visited.has(neighbors[i]!)) {\n        stack.push(neighbors[i]!);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  return order;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\n0 → [1,2], 1 → [3]\nOne possible DFS order: [0,1,3,2].\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\nTime O(V + E).\nSpace O(V) worst case.\n\nStep 4 — Interview rule:\nUse recursion when depth is controlled and clarity matters; use an explicit stack when recursion depth can be large.",
