    title: "Topological sorting",
    difficulty: "Medium",
    experienceLevel: "2–5 Years",
    category: "Graphs",
    expectedAnswer: "A topological order is a linear ordering of vertices in a directed acyclic graph (DAG) such that every edge u→v places u before v. Kahn's algorithm repeatedly removes zero-indegree vertices.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Compute indegrees.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction topologicalSort(\n  vertexCount: number,\n  graph: readonly (readonly number[])[],\n): number[] {\n  const indegree = new Array<number>(vertexCount).fill(0);\n\n  for (let node = 0; node < vertexCount; node++) {\n    for (const next of graph[node] ?? []) {\n      indegree[next]++;\n    }\n  }\n\n  const queue: number[] = [];\n\n  for (let node = 0; node < vertexCount; node++) {\n    if (indegree[node] === 0) {\n      queue.push(node);\n    }\n  }\n\n  const order: number[] = [];\n  let front = 0;\n\n  while (front < queue.length) {\n    const node = queue[front++]!;\n    order.push(node);\n\n    for (const next of graph[node] ?? []) {\n      indegree[next]--;\n\n      if (indegree[next] === 0) {\n        queue.push(next);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  return order.length === vertexCount ? order : [];\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN — Map-based graph:\n```ts\nfunction topologicalSortUsingMap(\n  graph: Map<string, string[]>,\n): string[] {\n  const indegree = new Map<string, number>();\n\n  for (const node of graph.keys()) {\n    indegree.set(node, 0);\n  }\n\n  for (const neighbors of graph.values()) {\n    for (const next of neighbors) {\n      indegree.set(next, (indegree.get(next) ?? 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n\n  const queue = [...indegree]\n    .filter(([, degree]) => degree === 0)\n    .map(([node]) => node);\n\n  const result: string[] = [];\n\n  while (queue.length > 0) {\n    const node = queue.shift()!;\n    result.push(node);\n\n    for (const next of graph.get(node) ?? []) {\n      const degree = indegree.get(next)! - 1;\n      indegree.set(next, degree);\n\n      if (degree === 0) queue.push(next);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return result.length === indegree.size ? result : [];\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\nA → C\nB → C\nC → D\nPossible order: A, B, C, D.\n\nStep 3 — Cycle detection:\nIf result.length < V, at least one cycle exists.\n\nStep 4 — Complexity:\nO(V + E) time and O(V) extra space.",
