    expectedAnswer: "Shortest-path strategy depends on edge weights. BFS is enough for unweighted graphs, Dijkstra handles non-negative weighted edges, and Bellman-Ford can handle negative edges. Choosing the algorithm from the graph's weight constraints is the key interview step.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — For an unweighted graph, use BFS.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction shortestUnweightedPath(\n  start: number,\n  graph: readonly (readonly number[])[],\n): number[] {\n  const distance = new Array<number>(graph.length).fill(-1);\n  const queue = [start];\n  let front = 0;\n\n  distance[start] = 0;\n\n  while (front < queue.length) {\n    const node = queue[front++]!;\n\n    for (const next of graph[node] ?? []) {\n      if (distance[next] !== -1) continue;\n\n      distance[next] = distance[node]! + 1;\n      queue.push(next);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return distance;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN-friendly:\n```ts\nfunction buildPath(\n  previous: readonly (number | null)[],\n  start: number,\n  target: number,\n): number[] {\n  const path: number[] = [];\n\n  for (let node: number | null = target; node !== null; node = previous[node]) {\n    path.push(node);\n    if (node === start) break;\n  }\n\n  path.reverse();\n\n  return path[0] === start ? path : [];\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\n0 → 1 → 2\n0 → 3 → 2\nBFS distance from 0:\n0=0, 1=1, 3=1, 2=2.\n\nStep 3 — Decision table:\n- Unweighted → BFS.\n- Non-negative weighted → Dijkstra.\n- Negative edges → Bellman-Ford.\n- All-pairs with suitable constraints → Floyd-Warshall.\n\nStep 4 — Complexity:\nBFS O(V+E). The chosen weighted algorithm determines the weighted case.",
