    expectedAnswer: "Frontend dependency relationships form directed graphs: modules/packages are vertices and imports/dependencies are directed edges. Topological ordering, cycle detection, and reachability are common graph operations for build and tooling problems.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Model dependencies:\nIf A imports B, use edge A → B or choose the opposite convention consistently.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ntype DependencyGraph = Record<string, string[]>;\n\nfunction dependencyOrder(graph: DependencyGraph): string[] {\n  const indegree: Record<string, number> = Object.create(null);\n\n  for (const node of Object.keys(graph)) {\n    indegree[node] ??= 0;\n\n    for (const next of graph[node]!) {\n      indegree[next] = (indegree[next] ?? 0) + 1;\n    }\n  }\n\n  const queue: string[] = [];\n\n  for (const node of Object.keys(indegree)) {\n    if (indegree[node] === 0) {\n      queue.push(node);\n    }\n  }\n\n  const order: string[] = [];\n\n  let front = 0;\n\n  while (front < queue.length) {\n    const node = queue[front++]!;\n    order.push(node);\n\n    for (const next of graph[node] ?? []) {\n      indegree[next]!--;\n\n      if (indegree[next] === 0) {\n        queue.push(next);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  return order.length === Object.keys(indegree).length\n    ? order\n    : [];\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN — Map/Set cycle-safe graph indexing:\n```ts\nfunction buildDependencyIndex(\n  dependencies: readonly [string, string][],\n): Map<string, Set<string>> {\n  const graph = new Map<string, Set<string>>();\n\n  for (const [moduleName, dependency] of dependencies) {\n    if (!graph.has(moduleName)) {\n      graph.set(moduleName, new Set<string>());\n    }\n\n    graph.get(moduleName)!.add(dependency);\n  }\n\n  return graph;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\nA → B\nA → C\nB → D\nC → D\nA valid build order can be A, B, C, D.\n\nStep 3 — Cycle:\nA → B → C → A means no valid topological build order exists.\n\nStep 4 — Production takeaway:\nReal bundlers and package managers have additional concerns such as duplicate modules, dynamic imports, plugins, caching, parallel work, and incremental builds.",
